-*- mode: outline -*-

* For 1.0

 - Ha, like this would ever be "done".  At this point, it's just a running joke
   to have the major version be zero.

   RuckusRD has been stable enough to have been released as 1.0 for over 15
   years... perhaps we should just drop the leading 0 and do a v25.0 release
   after v0.24 or something like that...?


* For 0.x

 - Consider adding `rescue` to kick-off a dialog-based rootfs recovery of some
   sort?  Or maybe just add a /bin/rescue script to run via `initramsys` shell?

 - Udev rules to rename network interfaces like systemd, so pre-boot network
   config can actually have the right device names?

   https://systemd.io/PREDICTABLE_INTERFACE_NAMES/
   https://wiki.archlinux.org/title/Network_configuration#Change_interface_name

 - Hmm... looks like the eudev project is dead.  At some point we'll have to
   start building just the bits of systemd we want (e.g., udev, systemd-boot)
   and firgure out a path forward.  Hopefully something like this will do:

   meson setup build -Dmode=minimal -Dlink-udev-shared=false -Dboot=true \
    -Dcore=false -Dnetworkd=false -Dresolved=false -Dhomed=false \
    -Duserdb=false -Dtimedated=false -Dlocaled=false

   ninja -C build && ninja -C build install

   Keep in mind, we've got at least one initramfs prog that links against the
   host system's libsystemd.so and also libnss_systemd.so, so we'll have to
   watch out for library mismatches... :-/

 - syslinux looks dead as a doornail as well.  that's sad.  there do seem to be
   some patched versions in distros, that may support dual EFI/BIOS booting via
   syslinux/isolinux... dunno if that's worth investigating.  I can't imagine
   using anything other than extlinux for BIOS systems, but we shuld probably
   start playing with systemd-boot for EFI booting

   I've played around with systemd-boot a bit.  Config is broken up into lots
   of little files instead of one, which seems annoyingly
   overcomplicated... but I suppose I can get used to it.  At least it's a
   project with a future.  It does not, however, support booting the xen.gz
   multiboot binary... which is unfortunate, becuase getting xen.efi to boot
   can be a PITA on some systems (e.g., I've actualy never gotten it to boot on
   my Threadripper box).  It looks like grub is the only mainstream, maintained
   bootloader that will do that...

   I've done some playing with grub now after decades of ignoring its annoying
   distro auto-gen config file nonsense.  I was able to quite easily get xen.gz
   booted via multiboot2 and xen.efi via chainloader, so that does offer an
   obvious standard boot approach... but I loathe the grub-mkconfig setup.
   It's trying way too hard to do everything for everyone and as a result just
   does it poorly.  I'll probably generate a hand updatable custom grub.cfg and
   attempt to disable update-grub and grub-mkconfig (although that will surely
   get undone by distro updates, maybe I can add a grub.d rule to just bomb out
   really early?)

 - maybe play with erofs to see how it compairs to squashfs?


* proposed for 0.24?

 - maybe allow NFS/SMB for imgdir in system_installer?  could be handy,
   probably wouldn't be hard, assuming we have NFS or SMB tools available in
   initramsys...

   looks like i can `mount -ousername=,domainname=,password=
   //hostname/sharename t` from initramsys, but I have to specify password on
   commandline.  there's no password helper prompt.

   i feel like nfs should be working, but when i try it hangs forever.  i can
   see the server has authenticated the mount request... it just never
   finishes?  i'm having strange networking behavior on this test, so perhaps
   i'll reboot and try again.

   that may have been because lo was down.  with it up i imediately get
   "Connection refused", even though i can see the server has granted me
   permission.  dmesg shows 'svc:failed to register lockdv1 RPC service (error
   111)'.  ok, i can '-o nolock' but perhaps that's bad.  otherwise i have to
   start the rpc.statd (and rpcbind?) sevice.  using -o nolock is fine for ro
   mounts.

 - Why can't I configure EFI bootloader when installing on Minisforum MS1-01?
   Is it a syslinux bug, or an efibootmgr bug, or just a bug in the EFI
   firmware itself?  I can install grub just fine after booting up off rescue
   media, and it works... i haven't tried configuring syslinux again post-boot,
   but it sure didn't work when done via the initramsys_installer... so could
   be something borky in our version of efibootmgr?  We are using an ancient
   version of efibootmgr, because of bionic build problems... might be time to
   finally disown bionic :-/

 - abandon bionic support and upgrade dropbear, efibootmgr, groff, fsarchiver,
   and enable rsync manpage.  can also abandon bionic support in firmwarenator
   and use zstd there (and upgrade firwarenator version in ruckusrd).

 - I've seen an ash error a couple times, perhaps just on really old debian
   boxes?  The host os shouldn't matter, though, it's coming out of initramfs.

   ash: .: unknown operand

   But then it boots fine...?  Definately saw it with my 6.18.44 kernel package
   w/ initrd built with v0.22.3... oh and that initrd doesn't have any
   microcode in it (I built it on feanor, I think... which must not have
   ucode.img in /boot).  I wonder if the lack of microcode is somehow related
   to the ash error...?

 - Consider having installer actually populate a root ZFS dataset with contents
   of requested overlay, insted of booting the overlay os rootfs.  would make
   installation much slower, perhaps add an experimental "DEEP=y" option or
   something like that.  The only real performance penalty of OverlayFS is
   initial copy-up of files being modified from a lower layer, but that penalty
   should drop from unnoticable to almost negligible over time.  Then we're
   just wasting a bit of disk space holding onto old .sqsh images.

   We do have to worry about these "hidden" limitations, though:

     1) OverlayFS doesn't support POSIX ACLs (or was that squashfs).

     2) The whoe /mnt/root-true layered rootfs is confusing to users.

     3) Anything not-ubuntu isn't getting implicitly tested by Ubuntu's
        zillions of users... so surely we will eventually run into other
        strange bugs.

   We could alternatively/additionally consider installing a tool to do this
   after installation, although that will be quite awkward.  All rootfs
   maintenance is awkward, and this would essentially be recreating rootfs
   entirely... so I guess the "tool" would need to be done inside initramsys,
   either manually or automatically via a special 'initramsys_unsquashfs' or
   'initramsys_unlayer' flag.


* proposed for 0.23

 - try and make sure ucode.img actually gets installed... i keep forgetting to
   do it on my kernel build box, so while i'm developing and creating initrd in
   the src dir, i get new firmware... but one installing ruckusrd via apt it's
   going back to old firmware.

   maybe i should make it complain if /boot/ucode.img is missing or older than
   6 months?  or older than the ruckusrd release date?

 - Creating custom fw.sqsh during install, then assuming it's ALL the firmware
   the kernel will need post-boot, and mounting it at /lib/firmware, can result
   in missing firmware for later hot-plugged devices.  Like network devices
   that don't initialize until they've got link... same is probably true for
   some video card firmware.  to fix this corner-case... we'll have to replace
   the small fw.sqsh with a giant one?  then boot up and fully initialize
   everything we need and recreate small fw.sqsh?

   Was this just a bad assumption to begin with?  The leaving fw.sqsh mounted
   part, I mean...  The original intent of FWDEV was to make sure early-loaded
   modules early-loading firmware would work...  once we hand off to init,
   shouldn't we let the remaining fw live on rootfs?  We could *inject*
   initrd-stage fw into rootfs, to make sure it's there, too, but even that
   might be a stretch?

   Does mod_inject cause fw injection at all, or just kernel modules and
   headers?

 - Migrate system_installer to grub, perhaps only experimentally?  could do a
   boot flag to control it... but we may as well just jump in with 2 feet.
   extlinux is bios only, syslinux only sorta works efi, and the project has
   been abandoned by its maintainer for years.

   This will mean we no longer need a "larger than usual" ESP, because now we
   can have ESP and /boot be different filesystems, and that means we can use a
   propper filesystem for /boot (so symlinks will work again).  Probably still
   want /boot to be a different ZFS dataset, so it can have different snapshots
   than rootfs.
